'''Inforg''' is Floridi's concept of the informational organism, proposed in his philosophy of information as a way to reconceptualize human identity and agency within an environment increasingly mediated by information and communication technologies. Its primary scope is philosophical anthropology and the relationship between informational entities and the [[Infosphere/Technical|infosphere]], rather than a scientific taxonomy of life.

== Intellectual provenance ==
Floridi's 2007 article contrasts an increasingly connected informational existence with the image of a technologically augmented human body. The 2008 article on artificial companions places the concept in a broader interpretation of historical changes in human self-understanding, associating the information revolution with Alan Turing. ''The Fourth Revolution'' (2014) further develops the argument about informational environments and human identity. These works articulate an interpretive framework and technological forecasts rather than demonstrating a necessary evolutionary transition.

